<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<object type="traktor.mcp.SkillAsset">
	<name>particle-effect</name>
	<description>Author a Spray particle EffectData (clone an example, recolor the particle shader, tune source/modifiers/loop).</description>
	<whenToUse><![CDATA[Need a particle effect — blood, sparks, smoke, dust, fire, magic — as a reusable EffectData asset, whether played by an EffectComponent or spawned via an event.]]></whenToUse>
	<body><![CDATA[# Author a Spray particle effect

Create a `traktor.spray.EffectData` "{{name}}". Authoring an emitter graph from scratch is fiddly, because it needs a point-sprite **shader** to render. **Clone a working example and tweak it** instead.

## Start by cloning an example
The engine ships ready effects under `System/Examples/Spray`, each with a valid particle shader under `System/Examples/Spray/Shaders`:
- `Example - Point` `{023214E0-E75D-DF43-BB51-76D8A26970A6}`: omni point burst, the best general starting point.
- `Example - Nozzle`, `Example - Surface`, `Example - XZ`: directional, surface and planar variants.

`clone_instance` it to your project (e.g. `Effects/{{name}}`). The clone already has one `EffectLayerData` → `EmitterData` → `PointSourceData` + an `IntegrateModifier` + a shader. The examples LOOP (`loopEnd` 1).

## Structure (what you are tuning)
- **EffectData**: `duration`, `loopStart`, `loopEnd`, `layers[]`.
  - **`loopStart == loopEnd` (e.g. both 0) = one-shot**: plays `0..duration`, then `isFinished`.
  - Otherwise it loops the `loopStart..loopEnd` time range (seconds) forever.
- **EffectLayerData**: `name`, `time` (start offset), `duration` (how long this layer emits), `emitter`.
- **EmitterData**:
  - `source`, `modifiers[]`;
  - `shader` (`Id<Shader>`, the point-sprite material);
  - `worldSpace` (default true: particles stay in the world as the emitter moves);
  - `gpu` (default true; see Gotchas);
  - `capacity` (default 1000), `sort`, cull/`middleAge` tuning.
- **SourceData**: the base has `constantRate` (particles/sec) and `velocityRate` (extra per emitter speed).
  - `PointSourceData` adds `position` and the `Range` fields `velocity`, `orientation`, `angularVelocity`, `age` (lifetime, sec), `mass`, `size`.
  - Other sources: `Cone` (adds `normal`, `angle1/2` for a directional spray), `Disc`, `Box`, `Sphere`, `Line`, `Quad`, `DirectionalPoint`.
- **Modifiers** (`layers[0].emitter.modifiers`):
  - `Integrate` is **required**: it advances position by velocity; without it particles never move.
  - Others: `Gravity`, `Drag`, `Size` (grow/shrink over life), `Brownian`, `CurlNoise`, `Vortex`, `PlaneCollision`.

## Recolor the particle (its own colour, not the surface)
The emitter `shader` is a full ShaderGraph: a `PixelState`, an External to the EmitterPoint fragment, an External to the world Output, and a `Color` node.
1. `clone_instance` the shader.
2. Find the Color node's index with `get_shader_graph` (`nN` = `nodes[N]`; it is n3 in `Point`, n2 in Nozzle/Surface/XZ), then set it: `set_instance_member { guid:<clonedShader>, member:"nodes[N].color", value:[r,g,b,a] }`.
3. Point `layers[0].emitter.shader` at the clone.

**The example shaders are additive** (`blendColorSource BfSourceAlpha`, `blendColorDestination BfOne`), so they can only brighten. For dark particles (blood, smoke, dust) set the PixelState node's `blendColorDestination` to `BfOneMinusSourceAlpha`. More shader editing: *shader-graph-authoring*.

## Typical tuning (blood-spurt example)
- `loopStart = 0`, `loopEnd = 0` (one-shot burst); `duration = 0.5` **and** `layers[0].duration = 0.5`. The layer keeps emitting for its own duration.
- `layers[0].emitter.shader` → the recolored, alpha-blended red shader.
- `...source.position = [0,1,0,1]` (emit at chest height), `...source.constantRate = 70`.
- `...source.velocity.max = 3`, `...source.age` `0.2..0.5` (short-lived). Set `Range` **leaves individually** (`velocity.min`, `velocity.max`); a compound can't be set whole.

Then `build_asset` the effect (its shader/mesh deps build with it).

## Use it
- Play continuously on an entity: a `traktor.spray.EffectComponentData { effect:<guid> }`. It auto-plays on spawn; script exposes `enable`, `loopEnable`, `finished` and `reset()`.
- Spawn on a trigger (hit/death): raise it through an `EventSetComponent`; see the *event-set-effects* skill (no auto-play, fires on demand).

## Gotchas (hard-won)
- **GPU emitters (`gpu = true`, the default) support only some parts.** Sources: Point, Box, Cone, Disc, Quad; the others (Sphere, Line, DirectionalPoint, …) emit nothing. Modifiers: Integrate, Gravity, Drag, Size, Brownian; CurlNoise, Vortex and PlaneCollision are silently ignored. Set `gpu = false` when you need the others.
- **No `Integrate` modifier ⇒ frozen particles** at the source.
- **`shader` is mandatory** to render; that's why cloning (which brings one) beats building blank.
- A looping effect never finishes: on an always-enabled `EffectComponent` it emits forever, and a looping effect spawned by an event lives until cancelled. Use `loopEnd = loopStart` for bursts.
- Keep `capacity` and `constantRate` sane; GPU emitters are cheap, but the manager and draw cost still scale with the live particle count.]]></body>
	<engineVersion/>
	<published>true</published>
	<parameters>
		<item type="traktor.mcp.SkillParameter">
			<name>name</name>
			<description>Effect name</description>
			<defaultValue>Blood</defaultValue>
		</item>
	</parameters>
</object>
